Relevant columns and their potential usage  
`body`: full article text – search for the word “education” (case–insensitive) or other phrases; can also be used to count total words or characters if needed.  
`title`: headline – a second place to look for the word “education” (some short pieces might mention it only in the title).  
`category`: high‑level topical section (e.g. “sport”, “politics”, “business”) – use it to group and aggregate counts.  
`filename`: unique file identifier – can be used as a surrogate article ID when counting distinct stories (e.g. COUNT(DISTINCT filename)).  

Typical conditions you might use  
•  Filtering by category: `WHERE category = 'sport'`  
•  Looking for the word education anywhere in the story:  
  `WHERE LOWER(body) LIKE '%education%'  OR  LOWER(title) LIKE '%education%'`  
•  Counting distinct stories: `COUNT(DISTINCT filename)`  

Below are 10 progressively richer SELECT‑only queries (each capped with `LIMIT 20`) that explore the dataset and culminate in the required breakdown of totals vs. “education” mentions.

```sql
--Description: 1) Peek at 20 raw rows to understand basic fields
SELECT `filename`, `category`, `title`, `body`
FROM `bigquery-public-data.bbc_news.fulltext`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 2) List all distinct news categories that exist in the corpus
SELECT DISTINCT `category`
FROM `bigquery-public-data.bbc_news.fulltext`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 3) Fetch 20 articles whose body text contains the word “education”
SELECT `filename`, `category`, `title`
FROM `bigquery-public-data.bbc_news.fulltext`
WHERE LOWER(`body`) LIKE '%education%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 4) Basic count of total articles per category (top 20 categories)
SELECT `category`,
       COUNT(DISTINCT `filename`) AS total_articles
FROM `bigquery-public-data.bbc_news.fulltext`
GROUP BY `category`
ORDER BY total_articles DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 5) Count of articles that mention “education” somewhere in body or title, by category
SELECT `category`,
       COUNT(DISTINCT `filename`) AS edu_articles
FROM `bigquery-public-data.bbc_news.fulltext`
WHERE LOWER(`body`)   LIKE '%education%'
   OR LOWER(`title`)  LIKE '%education%'
GROUP BY `category`
ORDER BY edu_articles DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 6) Combine total vs. education counts for each category via two subqueries and a JOIN
SELECT
  tot.category,
  tot.total_articles,
  COALESCE(edu.edu_articles, 0) AS edu_articles
FROM (
  SELECT `category`, COUNT(DISTINCT `filename`) AS total_articles
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.bbc_news.fulltext`
  GROUP BY `category`
) AS tot
LEFT JOIN (
  SELECT `category`, COUNT(DISTINCT `filename`) AS edu_articles
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.bbc_news.fulltext`
  WHERE LOWER(`body`) LIKE '%education%' OR LOWER(`title`) LIKE '%education%'
  GROUP BY `category`
) AS edu
ON tot.category = edu.category
ORDER BY tot.total_articles DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 7) Same as #6 but now calculate the percentage of education‑related stories
SELECT
  tot.category,
  tot.total_articles,
  COALESCE(edu.edu_articles, 0) AS edu_articles,
  SAFE_DIVIDE(COALESCE(edu.edu_articles,0), tot.total_articles) * 100 AS pct_education
FROM (
  SELECT `category`, COUNT(DISTINCT `filename`) AS total_articles
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.bbc_news.fulltext`
  GROUP BY `category`
) AS tot
LEFT JOIN (
  SELECT `category`, COUNT(DISTINCT `filename`) AS edu_articles
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.bbc_news.fulltext`
  WHERE LOWER(`body`) LIKE '%education%' OR LOWER(`title`) LIKE '%education%'
  GROUP BY `category`
) AS edu
ON tot.category = edu.category
ORDER BY pct_education DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 8) Use a WITH clause for readability, keep only categories with ≥50 stories
WITH source AS (
  SELECT `category`,
         COUNT(DISTINCT `filename`) AS total_articles,
         COUNT(DISTINCT IF(LOWER(`body`) LIKE '%education%' OR LOWER(`title`) LIKE '%education%', `filename`, NULL)) AS edu_articles
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.bbc_news.fulltext`
  GROUP BY `category`
)
SELECT category,
       total_articles,
       edu_articles,
       SAFE_DIVIDE(edu_articles, total_articles) * 100 AS pct_education
FROM source
WHERE total_articles >= 50
ORDER BY pct_education DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 9) Show categories with the highest raw count of education articles (top 20)
SELECT `category`,
       COUNT(DISTINCT `filename`) AS edu_articles
FROM `bigquery-public-data.bbc_news.fulltext`
WHERE LOWER(`body`) LIKE '%education%' OR LOWER(`title`) LIKE '%education%'
GROUP BY `category`
ORDER BY edu_articles DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 10) FINAL ANSWER – full breakdown of total articles, number & percentage mentioning “education”, sorted by category name
SELECT
  category,
  total_articles,
  edu_articles,
  ROUND(SAFE_DIVIDE(edu_articles, total_articles) * 100, 2) AS pct_education
FROM (
  SELECT `category`,
         COUNT(DISTINCT `filename`) AS total_articles,
         COUNT(DISTINCT IF(LOWER(`body`) LIKE '%education%' OR LOWER(`title`) LIKE '%education%', `filename`, NULL)) AS edu_articles
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.bbc_news.fulltext`
  GROUP BY `category`
)
ORDER BY category
LIMIT 20;
```

Each query is limited to 20 rows so they are safe to run interactively; you can remove or raise the `LIMIT` once satisfied with the logic to return the full result set.
